%% Curvature on a torus: 3-D figures drawn by the GPU renderer (View(renderer="gpu")),
%% illustrated like a paper figure, with exact vector stills for print and PDF.
%%   lmr2svg examples/torus/deck.lmr -o torus.html
!title Curvature on a Torus
!madewith
!subtitle shape, frames, curvature and geodesics
!author lemur examples
!theme journal

!slide A torus, from every side

!columns[64 36]
	!column
		!anim
			!src shape.py
	!column[.center]
		A torus is not convex: it hides parts of itself, differently from
		every side.

		The figure is drawn with a depth buffer on the GPU, so the shading, the
		outline and the hidden parts stay right while the camera goes round,
		and even under it.

!slide Coordinates and the tangent plane

!columns[64 36]
	!column
		!anim
			!src frame.py
	!column[.center]
		A **meridian** ($u$ fixed) and a **parallel** ($v$ fixed) meet at $p$.

		:: math
			T(u, v) = \begin{pmatrix} (R + r\cos v)\cos u \\ (R + r\cos v)\sin u \\ r \sin v \end{pmatrix}

		Their velocities $T_u$ and $T_v$ span the tangent plane $T_pM$.

!slide Curvature changes sign

!columns[64 36]
	!column
		!anim
			!src curvature.py
	!column[.center]
		:: math
			K = \frac{\cos v}{r\,(R + r\cos v)}

		[Positive]{#c8562a} outside, [negative]{#2f6aa8} in the hole, zero on the
		two circles where the torus is flat in one direction.

!slide Geodesics from one point

!columns[64 36]
	!column
		!anim
			!src geodesics.py
	!column[.center]
		A geodesic is a curve that does not turn **within** the surface:

		:: math
			\ddot u &= \frac{2 r \sin v}{R + r\cos v}\,\dot u\,\dot v \\
			\ddot v &= -\frac{R + r\cos v}{r}\,\sin v\;\dot u^2

		Some wind round the outside forever; others dive through the hole.
